def rectangle_intersection_area(r1, r2):
    left = max(r1[0], r2[0])
    right = min(r1[2], r2[2])
    bottom = max(r1[1], r2[1])
    top = min(r1[3], r2[3])
    if left < right and bottom < top:
        return (right - left) * (top - bottom)
    else:
        return 0
```

The area of the intersection is the product of the widths and heights of its axis-aligned bounding box, and that bounding box exists only when the rectangles overlap. Taking the max of the left boundaries and the min of the right boundaries (and similarly for the top and bottom boundaries) defines that bounding box. If the left edge is not strictly to the left of the right edge, or the bottom edge is not strictly below the top, the bounding box has no interior and the area is zero. Two rectangles that touch only along a shared edge or corner therefore have an intersection of area zero, which is what the specification asks for. I have not relied on any particular order of the rectangle arguments because the max and min operations are symmetric. The code returns 1 for the first example and 0 for the other two, both of which agree with the checks.

