def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    px, py = p
    ax, ay = a
    bx, by = b

    if ay == by:
        # Horizontal segment; p must lie on the same horizontal line and between the x-coordinates
        return py == ay and min(ax, bx) <= px <= max(ax, bx)
    elif ax == bx:
        # Vertical segment; p must lie on the same vertical line and between the y-coordinates
        return px == ax and min(ay, by) <= py <= max(ay, by)
    else:
        # General case: compute the two parametrised positions and require them to agree
        # Parametrise p - a = t (b - a) and solve for t
        t1 = (px - ax) / (bx - ax)
        t2 = (py - ay) / (by - ay)
        # t1 and t2 must be equal and lie in [0, 1]
        return abs(t1 - t2) < 1e-8 and (0 <= t1 <= 1)
```

The function checks whether p lies on the segment from a to b by comparing its coordinates with each endpoint. When the segment is horizontal or vertical, it verifies the other coordinate lies in the appropriate range. For a general sloped segment, it derives two versions of the parameter t along the segment from both coordinates and accepts p only when those versions are equal, within a small tolerance, and t lies between 0 and 1. Endpoints are included by requiring t no larger than 1, even though t = 1 corresponds exactly to the second endpoint.

