def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    x, y = p
    ax, ay = a
    bx, by = b
    if ax == bx:
        return (x == ax) and (min(ay, by) <= y <= max(ay, by))
    if ay == by:
        return (y == ay) and (min(ax, bx) <= x <= max(ax, bx))
    dx = bx - ax
    dy = by - ay
    return (dy * (x - ax) == dx * (y - ay)) and (min(ax, bx) <= x <= max(ax, bx)) and (min(ay, by) <= y <= max(ay, by))
```

The function checks whether p lies on the line segment from a to b in two steps. For the first step, if the segment is horizontal, p must share the same y-coordinate and sit between its endpoints' y-values; for a vertical segment, p must share the same x-coordinate and sit between the x-values. If the segment is neither horizontal nor vertical, p lies on the line when its coordinates satisfy the cross-product formula for lines, and it is itself in the axis ranges that define the segment. The endpoint checks are included in a <= b comparisons, so two identical endpoints also form a valid segment containing either point.

The cross-product test alone would return True for any point on the infinite line through a and b, so the axis bounds are needed to reject points outside the segment. None of the arithmetic depends on particular coordinates, and making sure True and False are the only results outside the bounds shows that both endpoints are included as requested.

