```

The function relies on one property: a polygon is convex if and only if, when it is closed by repeating the first point at the end, every triple of consecutive vertices turns consistently in the same direction. 'turn' here means cross product of the side from the middle point to the next and the side from the middle point to the previous one (X_{i+1} - X_i) cross (X_{i-1} - X_i). On the ring, interpreted cyclically, that flips the sign of the direction and never vanishes, so it equals twice the winding number of the triangle formed by each three vertices. When negated, the alternating-cumulative sign of the direction around the polygon never changes, characters mattering: zero signs are skipped, because exact collinearity is disallowed by the task.

The code uses a helper that cross-multiplies adjacent pairs out of two equal-length lists. For each triple of vertices (p, q, r), it crosses [p, q, r] with [p, r, q], the middle point fixed, working through the list as a ring with indexed values taken modulo n. For triangles, the ring reduction returns a positive quantity if those three points are ordered counter-clockwise. For polygons, the sign of the first non-zero cross value must equal all later non-zero cross values, otherwise the turns reverse and the polygon cups inward. The return value is True when no pair of repeated convex hull projections from adjacent triples overlaps with one from further ahead. Since all triples are in left-to-right order, a voter would order points according to which convex hull it projects on. The runtime is quadratic in the number of points.

