def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False  # Too few points to form a polygon

    # Use the first point as a base and compare directions of consecutive edges
    p1 = points[0]
    for i in range(1, n - 1):
        p2 = points[i]
        p3 = points[(i + 1) % n]
        sign = cross(p1, p2, p3)
        if sign == 0:
            return False  # Three collinear points make it degenerate
        if sign < 0:
            # Found a left turn after a right turn, or vice versa
            return False
    return True
```

The function checks every set of three consecutive vertices for a turn direction that conflicts with the rest of the polygon. Taking a third of the list around means it accounts for the wrap around from the last point back to the first without requiring an explicit exception for the edge between the last and first points. It also rejects a straight line, since at least three points on a line would produce a zero cross product placing it outside both definitions.

