def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(a, b, c):
        """Return the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac."""
        return (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        a, b = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n]
        c = points[(i + 2) % n]
        sign = cross(a, b, c)
        if sign == 0:
            return False  # All collinear points are allowed only for degenerate polygons, which are not considered convex.
        if any(cross(a, b, points[j]) * sign < 0 for j in range(i + 3, n + 1)):
            return False
    return True
```

The function determines the turn made at each convex vertex, measured by the signed z-component of the cross product of that vertex with its next two neighbours. For a convex polygon, all those turn vectors must point in the same direction (all clockwise or all counter-clockwise), and no three consecutive points may lie on a straight line. The edge ordering is not required to be clockwise; a purely counter-clockwise polygon is convex and will still produce all positive or all negative results.

Degenerate polygons, where all the points lie on the same straight line, are excluded by the task, and the one line of code that checks for them, sign == 0, returns False. A single interior point lying on the line formed by two other adjacent vertices would falsify "all turns in the same direction" and also render the polygon non-simple, so it is detected correctly even through the shorthand. The general property that adjacent points keep turning in the same direction is equivalent to a polygon being convex and simple, which is what the task asks for.

Each vertex is treated in turn as the middle of its adjacent pair, so the starting point has no special case.

