def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(x, y, z):
        # Cross product of (y - x) and (z - x)
        return (y[0] - x[0]) * (z[1] - x[1]) - (y[1] - x[1]) * (z[0] - x[0])
    n = len(points)
    # Compare the sign of each of the n side-perpendicular dot products
    sign = None
    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        s = cross(a, b, c)
        if s == 0:
            # The points lie on a line
            return False
        if sign is None:
            sign = 1 if s > 0 else -1
        elif s * sign < 0:
            return False
    return True
```

The decision is made by the cross product rather than by a single direction. Walking around the polygon, (i - 1, i, i + 1) must turn all the same way, whether clockwise or counter-clockwise: once the sign of one angular change is established, every later change must have the same sign. A cross product of zero indicates a straight angle at a vertex, which negates the definition that requires all vertices to turn the same way, so a planar polygon, such as three collinear points, returns False. That matches the note that collinear vertices are not considered convex. Using consecutive indices and taking i + 2 modulo n makes the wrap from the last back to the first explicit for the final side.

The code is not tied to the example rectangles and triangles; its logic is general for any simple polygon with more than two points.

