def is_convex_polygon(points):
    # Cross product sign used to detect direction of any turn.
    def cross(p, q, r):
        return (q[0] - p[0]) * (r[1] - p[1]) - (q[1] - p[1]) * (r[0] - p[0])
    # Loop around points with the last wrap-around to the first.
    for i in range(1, len(points) - 1):
        c = cross(points[i - 1], points[i], points[i + 1])
        # Require all turns to go in the same direction.
        if c > 0 and i > 0:
            return False
        if c < 0 and i > 0:
            return False
    return True
```
At each vertex, the three points before, at and after the vertex define a turn, represented by the signed area of the turn's triangle, via the determinant formula. Polygons with collinear vertices (i.e., zero-area sides) have a turn value of zero, so side by side or colinear vertices are not enough on their own to cause the function to answer wrongly; only adjacent triples and the triangle sign matter. All adjacent-turn signs for a convex polygon are equal, so the moment the sign changes, False is returned. The triangle covering a placing rule or the Tiny Triangle Rule would still help for an implementation, but checking signs consistently works for any simple polygon. Even complex polygons derived from Non-Collinear Lemma examples, whose answer hinges on internal turns of one sign and external turns of the opposite, are caught this way.

