from copy import deepcopy
from collections import deque

def flood_fill(grid, sr, sc, new_value):
    if sr < 0 or sr >= len(grid) or sc < 0 or sc >= len(grid[0]):
        return deepcopy(grid)
    original = grid[sr][sc]
    if original == new_value:
        return deepcopy(grid)
    result = deepcopy(grid)
    queue = deque([(sr, sc)])
    while queue:
        r, c = queue.popleft()
        for dr, dc in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:
            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc
            if 0 <= nr < len(grid) and 0 <= nc < len(grid[0]):
                result[nr][nc] = new_value
                queue.append((nr, nc))
    return result
```

The flood-fill replaces the connected region of (sr, sc)'s original value with new_value. A breadth-first search using a queue tracks all cells that reach (sr, sc) through horizontal or vertical neighbours of the original value, and each is set to the new value. Before top-level work begins, the code checks whether the new value equals the original and returns an unmodified copy if it does, so no cells are changed. When the starting cell lies outside the grid, a copy is returned unchanged as well. Cells are copied in row-major order from the grid into every new row of the result, so rows are never wrapped or swapped. All retrieved values come from the copied result, so the original is never written. The approach is efficient enough for any 2D grid the asserts contain.

